{
"Democritus is the closest Greek parallel to the Abhidharma. His best-known fragment, in one common translation, reads: By convention sweet is sweet, bitter is bitter, hot is hot, cold is cold, color is color; but in truth there are only atoms and the void. The structure is the same as Vasubandhus two truths. Everyday things exist by agreement, and the analysis reveals what really exists. Both thinkers reach the real by taking things apart. The differences are large, though. Democritus atoms are eternal, unchanging, and purely physical, and the soul itself is made of fine atoms. The Abhidharmas ultimate elements include feelings, perceptions, and intentions, and they are events that last a moment. For Democritus, color is the thing that exists only by convention, and the atom is real. For the Abhidharma, the moment of seeing color is itself one of the real elements.": "6ebd3b9a822f",
"Descartes also said that existing now does not guarantee existing next. He concluded that God keeps re-creating us.": "0788be0e5dce",
"In the third Meditation Descartes made an argument about time that sounds like the Treasury. The whole time of my life may be divided into an infinity of parts, each of which is in no way dependent on any other; and, accordingly, because I was in existence a short time ago, it does not follow that I must now exist, unless in this moment some cause create me anew as it were, that is, conserve me. He added that conserving a thing requires the same power and act that would be necessary to create it. Like Vasubandhu, he denied that existing at one moment carries a thing into the next. The two drew opposite conclusions from it. Descartes concluded that God must re-create the world at every instant, and he used the argument to prove Gods existence. Vasubandhu concluded that each moment arises from the conditions of the moment before, with no creator and no lasting substance, and that this is all continuity has ever been.": "3b360e2cfd34",
"Its test: if the idea of a thing disappears when you break it or analyze it, the thing is conventional, like a pot or water.": "82fc88e85da8",
"On structure the closest is Democritus. Both divide what exists by convention from what exists in truth, and both find the truth by analysis. On time the closest is Descartes, who saw as clearly as Vasubandhu that existing now does not guarantee existing next. On composite objects the closest is the mereological nihilist, for whom a pot is only its pieces. Each of these Western thinkers shares one part of the Abhidharma picture, and none of them shares all of it.": "b90af56c586b",
"Some contemporary philosophers defend a view called mereological nihilism, which says that objects made of parts do not exist at all. There are no chairs, only simple particles arranged chairwise. The American philosopher Peter van Inwagen, in Material Beings, defended a version very close to this, with one exception. He argued that living organisms are real wholes, because the activity of their parts makes up a single life. The nihilists pot and the Treasurys pot are the same: nothing in the world is a pot over and above the pieces, and the word is a convenience. The exception is where the comparison becomes sharp. Van Inwagen kept the living being, and in particular the human being, as a real whole. For the Abhidharma the person is the clearest example of a conventional thing, Vajirās chariot. The Buddhist analysis was aimed at the self first and at pots only for practice.": "222c88dcfc79",
"The Abhidharma took experience apart into its smallest elements, called dharmas.": "806523ef714a",
"The analysis starts in the suttas. When Māra asks the nun Vajirā who created the sentient being, she answers, in Bhikkhu Sujatos translation, This is just a pile of conditions, you wont find a sentient being here. When the parts are assembled we use the word chariot. So too, when the aggregates are present sentient being is the convention we use. The Abhidharma, the third basket of the canon, made this a system. It listed the elements, the dharmas, that remain when everything made of parts has been taken apart. Vasubandhus Treasury of Abhidharma, translated by Xuanzang, gives the test: If the idea of a thing disappears when it is broken, or when the mind analyzes it into other things, it is conventional, like a pot or water. What is not like this is ultimate. Break a pot and nothing in the pieces is a pot. Analyze water into color, smell, taste, and touch, and nothing is left that is water. Color itself, or a feeling, survives any analysis, and so it counts as ultimately real.": "93c3102fc7b0",
"The analysis was meant to be watched in meditation, and its main target was the self.": "a2d24b6631c1",
"The elements are not lasting things. The Sautrāntika and Sarvāstivāda schools disagreed about much, and both held that every conditioned element is momentary. The Treasury defines the term: a moment is ceasing immediately upon obtaining its existence. Conditioned things, as soon as they come into being, immediately perish. The masters of Abhidharma even measured it: In the time it takes a strong man to snap his fingers once, there are sixty-five moments. Vasubandhu drew a striking conclusion about motion. If a thing perishes where it arises, nothing ever travels from one place to another. What we call movement is a series of new elements arising in neighboring places, the way a row of lamps seems to carry a light along.": "823b28e6a77f",
"The elements include feelings and thoughts, not only matter, and each lasts only a moment.": "5c2c495eef55",
"The first further step is that the analysis is meant to be watched. Democritus could not see his atoms, and nobody can observe Descartes moments of re-creation. The Abhidharma elements are things like a feeling, a sound, an intention, and a trained attention can catch them as they arise and pass. The insight traditions of both Theravāda and Tibet teach exactly this: watch the rise and fall closely enough, and the sense of a solid, lasting observer begins to loosen. The claim that a moment is one sixty-fifth of a finger snap should not be read as physics. It says that the arising and passing is much faster than ordinary attention, and that training can bring attention closer to it. Modern readers sometimes compare momentariness to quantum physics or to film frames. The comparisons can help a beginner, and the teaching does not rest on them.": "526d50238a8b",
"The likeness ends at the purpose. Democritus wanted to explain nature, Descartes wanted to prove God, and the nihilists want the simplest true account of what there is. The Abhidharma analysis was meant to be seen in meditation as well as believed. The practitioner watches feelings and thoughts arise and pass, and sees that nothing in them stays long enough to be a self or to be worth clinging to. Vajirās verse ends this way: its only suffering that comes to be, lasts a while, then expires. The list of elements was a map for that seeing. Later Buddhists, beginning with Vasubandhus own critique of atoms, said that the elements too lack any fixed essence. That claim would make no sense in Democritus system, and it is the next step on the Buddhist path.": "16c868044766",
"The second further step is that Buddhist analysis did not stop at the elements. The Abhidharma treated dharmas as the ultimate truth, the place where analysis ends. Vasubandhus own argument against atoms showed that even the smallest unit of matter cannot be coherently conceived, and the Madhyamaka, in the lesson on emptiness, went further: every element, examined, depends on other things and has no fixed nature of its own. The tradition kept the Abhidharma anyway. It still trains monks and nuns in the lists, because the analysis does its work even if it is not the last word. It breaks the hold of the pot, the chariot, and the self, and then the student is ready to let go of the elements too. This is the raft again, from the first lesson of this shelf.": "a8031f28b196",
"Vasubandhu argued for momentariness, and did not simply assert it. His argument, examined in the next section, rests on the claim that destruction needs no cause. The same Vasubandhu later wrote the Twenty Verses on consciousness-only, and there he turned the analysis against the atoms that the Abhidharma had treated as the smallest units of matter. If an atom joins with six others, it must have six parts. If the six share its place, the whole heap would be the size of one atom. Either the atom has sides, and so is not the smallest thing, or it has none, and then nothing can be built from it. The man who wrote the great textbook of analysis also showed where analysis runs out.": "830e2b0adbcd",
"Vasubandhu argued that destruction needs no cause, so things perish as soon as they arise.": "f00c45aa222c",
"Westerners usually know atomism from school, so begin there. Democritus said that sweet and bitter exist by convention and atoms exist in truth. Then give Vasubandhus test, which is easy to picture: break the pot and the pot is gone, analyze the water and the water is gone. Point out the two differences. The Buddhist elements include the mind, and they last only a moment. The finger snap with sixty-five moments makes a memorable image, and you should say plainly that it is a measure of attention and not a laboratory figure. Then make the practical point. The analysis was meant to be seen, in meditation, and its target was the sense of a lasting self. End with Vajirās chariot, because it shows that the whole system grew out of a short verse spoken by a nun to Māra.": "c80e8a89c316"
}
